    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've noticed that when they're working hard, they make this high-pitched whistling or whining sound. Is there a mechanical reason for that specific noise, or is it just air moving?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The high-pitched whistling or whining sound from a turbocharger is primarily caused by the friction between the moving turbine blades and the compressor wheel's impeller, or \"volute\". This friction creates a audible whine due to the rapid acceleration of the compressing air and the rotation of the compressor. Some might call it a \"slow-rpm hiss\" or \"piston slap\" because it can also be related to excessive clearance or oil film thickness between the moving parts and the shafts, causing a noisy ranging effect.",
